//! The page's prose, for a reader with new eyes: one who knows neither
//! geometry nor machine learning nor X_eTaL. `[shown|id]` marks a word
//! that opens its plain explanation (docs/glossary.md, the pages'
//! glossary); `**words**` is bold. Kept apart from the page so a test
//! can check every mark names an idea the glossary has and no sentence
//! passes a verdict. No result lives here: the numbers are the run's.

pub const LEDE: &str = "A model's state moves a little at every step, and a rule says which way: a vector field. Five fields in the plane, drawn as arrows with the paths that follow them; two ways of following a field, one of which drifts off the circle it should stay on; what divergence and curl say about a field, and why a field that comes from a loss cannot circle. Then the comparison as machine learning meets it: gradient descent on a loss, which settles, beside two players each following their own slope of one objective, who spiral away from the answer, and two known repairs.";

pub const HINT: &str = "New to this? A word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation when you click it (or press Enter on it); click again to close it. The buttons pick which field to look at, the step size of the two integrators, and how many of their steps to show; picking runs nothing, every number was computed by the program at the bottom when the page opened.";

pub const QUESTION: [&str; 4] = [
    "A model's state moves a little at every step of training. What decides where it goes, and must it be heading downhill?",
    "Gradient descent says: downhill, on the loss. Each step goes against the loss's gradient, and the arrows of all those steps, drawn at every point, make a [gradient field|gradient-field]. But not every rule of motion in machine learning is downhill on anything. When two parts of a model pull against each other (a generator and a discriminator in a GAN, a generative adversarial network, are the known case) each follows its own slope, and the arrows of the pair need not come from any one function. They make a general [vector field|vector-field].",
    "This page draws both kinds in the plane, where a whole field fits on the screen: five fields made from a 2 by 2 matrix each, the paths that follow them, and two measurements that tell a field that comes from a loss from one that turns. Following a field is itself arithmetic with an error, so the page also measures two ways of doing it.",
    "Then it compares, on the smallest case there is: gradient descent on a loss beside a [two-player game|two-player-game] on one objective f(x, y) = x y, one player lowering it and the other raising it, from the same start at the same rate. Two known repairs for the game are measured beside them.",
];

pub const BACKGROUND: [&str; 6] = [
    "A model's **state** here is a point of the plane, two numbers (x, y). A rule of motion says, at every point, which way to move and how fast: a [vector field|vector-field], written dx/dt = v(x), which says that the rate of change of the state x over time t is the arrow v(x) at the state. Drawn as arrows on a grid it looks like wind on a weather map; a state set down anywhere drifts along the arrows, and the curve it traces is its **path** (a trajectory). In formulas such as dx/dt = v(x) and x + s v(x), x stands for the whole state, both of its numbers; where one coordinate is meant, the page writes (x, y).",
    "The five fields here are the simplest kind: v(x) = A x for a 2 by 2 matrix A = (a b / c d), written by rows, so the arrow at (x, y) is (a x + b y, c x + d y). Two numbers of A come back again and again: its **trace**, a + d, and its **determinant**, a d - b c (for the rotation (0 -1 / 1 0): trace 0, determinant 0 x 0 - (-1) x 1 = 1). I is the identity matrix (1 0 / 0 1), which leaves every point where it is. Every one of the fields has the arrow zero at x = 0: the [rest point|rest-point], where a state placed there stays. Fields this simple have exact paths, so the page can check every path it follows against the true one.",
    "A computer cannot follow a field continuously: it takes steps. The plainest, Euler's step, moves straight along the arrow where it starts, x + s v(x), for a step size s. A fourth-order Runge-Kutta step looks at the field four times within the step (at the start, twice half way, at the end) and averages what it saw. Both are [integrators|flow-integrator]; with steps of the same size they do not land in the same place.",
    "[Divergence and curl|divergence-curl] are two numbers a field has at each point: the divergence says whether the arrows spread out from it (positive) or gather into it (negative); the curl, whether they turn about it, anticlockwise counted positive. Both are found here by **central differences**: the field a small step dh to each side of the point, the difference divided by 2 dh, a slope estimated from two nearby values.",
    "The **gradient** of a function L at a point is the arrow of its slopes, one per coordinate: it points the way L rises fastest, and its length says how fast. A field is a [gradient field|gradient-field] when it is minus the gradient of one function L, a loss (in physics, a potential): at each point the arrow points the way L falls fastest, and gradient descent follows this field, in steps. Walk round a closed loop and add up v . dx along the way (the arrow dotted with each small piece of the walk: how much the walk went with the field). For a gradient field each piece adds the fall of L along it, and a loop ends where it began, so the total is zero. A field that turns has no such L, and its total is not zero: the page calls this [the sum round a loop|loop-sum].",
    "The game is the smallest [two-player game|two-player-game]: one number each, x for the first player and y for the second, and one objective f(x, y) = x y. The first wants f small, the second wants it large. Each steps along its own slope of f at a rate eta (the step size, called s for the integrators): the first down, x - eta y (the slope of x y along x is y), the second up, y + eta x. The point (0, 0) is the **solution** (an equilibrium): there, neither player can change f by moving alone.",
];

pub const RATIONALE: [&str; 3] = [
    "Keep the rule (follow the arrows) and change the field, and the paths do different things: fall in, circle, spiral in, come in along one line and leave along another, spiral out. What decides which is the matrix A, through two of its numbers, its trace and its determinant; the page computes what those predict and sets it beside what the paths did.",
    "Keep the field and change the way of following it, and the path changes too. On the rotation the exact paths are circles, so the distance from the circle is the error and can be read at every step: each straight Euler step ends outside the circle it started on, by a fixed factor; Runge-Kutta's steps stay near it.",
    "Gradient descent and the two-player game are both a rule of motion stepped at a rate. The loss gives a gradient field, which cannot circle. The game's two slopes together give the rotation, a field with no potential, which circles; stepped plainly, both players at once, the steps are Euler's on that rotation, and they should spiral out as Euler's do. If that is the mechanism, a repair should change how the field is stepped, not the field: the players taking turns, or a look-ahead step.",
];

pub const PURPOSE: [&str; 3] = [
    "An experiment on five formulas and one tiny game, not on a trained model: every field is linear, so every path, divergence, curl and loop sum has an exact value, and the page sets each measurement beside it. Every number, arrow and path comes from the X_eTaL program shown at the bottom, run in your browser when the page opened; the buttons choose which of its numbers you read and run nothing.",
    "These are illustrations in the plane and one small game, not a field learned from data. A field learned from data is part 2 of this page, still to come. The figures are marked [measured or analytical|measured-analytical]: the paths, the measures and the distances are measured; the exact paths and the unit circle are analytical, drawn from their formulas (every point of them still computed by the program).",
    "It compares two integrators on one field at four step sizes, and with the same work; and four rules from the same start at the same rate, one on a loss and three on the game. It does not show how often the training of a real GAN fails, or why: the game here has one number per player and a known answer.",
];

pub const INTUITION: [&str; 4] = [
    "Drop a leaf on a pond. Where the water drains toward a plughole the leaf goes in (an attractor); where it swirls, the leaf circles (a rotation); a swirl that drains spirals in; where two currents meet head on and leave sideways, the leaf comes in along one line and leaves along another (a saddle). The movement of the water is the field; the leaf's path is what following it does.",
    "A hill gives a field too: at every point, the way down. A ball rolling slowly down a hill cannot go round a loop and come back to where it started, because it would have to climb back up to its starting height. That is the difference between a gradient field and a general one: a gradient field has a height, and along its paths the height only falls.",
    "Two people share one dial: one wants the reading low, the other high, and each moves the dial a little toward what they want, watching only where it is now. Each answers the other's last move, and together they go round the setting where neither could gain by moving alone. If both move at once with moves a little too large, each round goes a little wider. That is the game below.",
    "Euler's step is walking round a circular track by looking at which way the track runs where you stand and walking straight for a few paces: every few paces you are a little outside the track, and the next straight walk starts from there, so you drift outward. Runge-Kutta's step looks at how the track turns ahead before taking the step.",
];

pub const MATHEMATICS: [&str; 7] = [
    "The field v(x) = A x with A = (a b / c d). The five: attractor (-1 0 / 0 -0.5), rotation (0 -1 / 1 0), spiral (-0.2 -1 / 1 -0.2), saddle (1 0 / 0 -1), expanding spiral (0.2 -1 / 1 0.2). The exact path from a start x0 is x(t) = exp(t A) x0, where exp(t A), the matrix exponential, is the matrix that carries any start along the field for a time t; it for a 2 by 2 matrix has a closed form the program writes out: with tau half the trace and B = A - tau I (B has trace 0, so B B = -det(B) I, det the determinant), exp(t A) = e^(tau t) (cos(w t) I + sin(w t) / w B) when det B > 0, with w = sqrt(det B), and the path turns; the same with cosh and sinh in place of cos and sin when det B < 0 (the hyperbolic cosine and sine, (e^u + e^-u) / 2 and (e^u - e^-u) / 2, which grow instead of turning), with w = sqrt(-det B); e^(tau t) (I + t B) when det B = 0.",
    "Euler's step: x + s v(x). The fourth-order Runge-Kutta step: k1 = v(x), k2 = v(x + s k1 / 2), k3 = v(x + s k2 / 2), k4 = v(x + s k3), then x + s (k1 + 2 k2 + 2 k3 + k4) / 6. On the rotation an Euler step takes (x, y) to (x - s y, y + s x), whose length is sqrt(1 + s^2) times the old length: every step multiplies the distance from the rest point by exactly that factor, so the path spirals out. A Runge-Kutta step multiplies it by the size of 1 + z + z^2 / 2 + z^3 / 6 + z^4 / 24 at z = i s (i the square root of -1), which is sqrt((1 - s^2 / 2 + s^4 / 24)^2 + (s - s^3 / 6)^2), just under 1: a very slow spiral in. First and fourth order describe how fast the error in the point itself shrinks with s, over a fixed time: in proportion to s for Euler, to s^4 for Runge-Kutta (on the rotation that error is mostly the point running ahead of or behind the exact one round the circle). The distance from the circle is a smaller part of it: Runge-Kutta's factor is about 1 - s^6 / 144 per step, so over the same number of steps its distance from the circle grows as s^6 (the table's 200 steps: doubling s multiplies it by about 64), and over a fixed time as s^5.",
    "The divergence is d vx / dx + d vy / dy and the curl d vy / dx - d vx / dy, where d vx / dx says how fast the arrow's x part changes as x changes. For v = A x both are the same at every point: the divergence is the trace a + d, the curl c - b. By central differences with step dh, d vx / dx is about (vx(x + dh, y) - vx(x - dh, y)) / 2 dh, exact for a linear field but for rounding.",
    "The sum round a loop: walk round a closed loop in straight pieces, dot the arrow at the middle of each piece with the piece, and add; for a linear field this is exactly the total of v . dx round the loop taken in infinitely small pieces (its integral). If v = -grad L (minus the gradient of L), each piece adds L at its start minus L at its end, so the loop adds 0. Green's theorem, a standard result, says that for any field the sum round a loop is the curl added up over the area inside, here the curl times the area; and, in its flux form, that the flow out across the loop (each piece's arrow dotted with the piece turned a quarter turn outward) is the divergence times the area. For a symmetric A (b = c), v = A x is minus the gradient of L(x) = -(a x^2 + 2 b x y + d y^2) / 2, and its curl c - b is 0; when b and c differ, no such L exists.",
    "Stability at the rest point x = 0. Along an [eigenvector|eigenvector] u of A (A u = lambda u, lambda its eigenvalue) the exact path is e^(lambda t) u: it shrinks if lambda is negative and grows if positive; a complex pair tau +- i w (a pair of eigenvalues that means the paths turn) turns at speed w, and its real part tau says whether they shrink or grow, as e^(tau t). So every path falls in if both eigenvalues have negative real parts, and some path leaves if either has a positive one. For a 2 by 2 matrix, written out from the trace T and the determinant D: lambda = T / 2 +- sqrt(T^2 / 4 - D); when T^2 / 4 < D the pair is complex, with real part T / 2. This needs no symmetry (a symmetric matrix has real eigenvalues; the turning fields are not symmetric).",
    "The game f(x, y) = x y. Player one's velocity is minus the slope of f along x, -y; player two's is plus the slope along y, x: together the field (-y, x), the rotation. Each rule is a fixed matrix M applied at every step, p <- M p, and its trace and determinant give the factor it multiplies the distance by. Simultaneous steps: M = (1 -eta / eta 1), Euler's step on the rotation, factor sqrt(1 + eta^2). Taking turns, the second player answering the first's new x: M = (1 -eta / eta 1 - eta^2), determinant 1 and trace 2 - eta^2, so its eigenvalues are complex of size 1 while eta < 2; the players circle, and x^2 + y^2 - eta x y stays fixed. The look-ahead step ([extragradient|extragradient]): both step to a trial point, then each steps from the start along its slope at the trial point, M = (1 - eta^2 -eta / eta 1 - eta^2), factor sqrt(1 - eta^2 + eta^4), under 1 while eta < 1. Gradient descent on L = (x^2 + y^2) / 2: M = (1 - eta) I.",
    "The program's words for these: u:e_uler and u:r_k4 (the two steps), u:p_ath (n steps), u:f_low (the exact exp(t A)), u:m_easures (divergence and curl by differences), u:l_oop and u:a_rea (the sum round a loop and Green's prediction), u:e_igen (the eigenvalues from the trace and determinant), u:s_imultaneous, u:t_urns and u:l_ookAhead (the game's rules). The integrators, the field measures and field pictures are general mathematics, asked of the sibling library X_eTaL-libraries (asks GL4, GL5 and GL6) and written out here meanwhile; nothing on this page is particular to manifolds, so none of it is in this repository's own libraries.",
];

pub const EXPERIMENT: [&str; 4] = [
    "What is measured. For each of the five fields: the arrows at a grid of points; the paths from four starts by Runge-Kutta, and the exact paths at the same times, with the largest gap between them, relative to the larger of 1 and the exact point's distance from the rest point; the divergence and the curl by central differences at every grid point against the exact values; the sum round a loop on the unit circle and the flow out across it, against the curl and the divergence times the area inside; the eigenvalues from the trace and the determinant, and each path's distance from the rest point at the end over its distance at the start.",
    "On the rotation, from a start on the unit circle: Euler's step and Runge-Kutta's at four step sizes, the same number of steps each, and the distance from the circle at every step, against the factor per step each is known to have. And the same work: Euler at the second step size against Runge-Kutta at the fourth, four times larger, over the same time, so both look at the field the same number of times.",
    "The game, from one start at one rate: gradient descent on L = (x^2 + y^2) / 2, simultaneous descent and ascent on f = x y, taking turns, and the look-ahead step; the distance from the solution (0, 0) at every step, each rule's matrix, and the factor its trace and determinant give.",
    "What was expected, written into the program's checks before it first ran, and what would have counted against it: the two symmetric fields (attractor and saddle) have a loop sum of 0 and the three turning fields do not; the sign of the largest real part says whether paths fall in or some leave; the Runge-Kutta paths stay within the preset's tolerance of the exact ones; Euler's distance from the circle grows by exactly its factor and Runge-Kutta's stays within 0.001 of it at step 0.1; with the same work Runge-Kutta ends nearer the circle; on the game, simultaneous steps move away from the solution while descent on the loss settles, taking turns stays within 2 of the solution and keeps x^2 + y^2 - eta x y fixed, and the look-ahead step settles too. A loop sum away from 0 for a symmetric field, a path leaving the attractor, or a repair that did not stop the growth would have counted against it.",
];

