pub const ANALYSIS: [&str; 2] = [
    "Read against what was expected. The two measurements told the fields that come from a loss from those that turn, as the mathematics says they must (for linear fields this is a check of the arithmetic, not a discovery). How a field is stepped changed the path too: on the rotation, whose exact paths neither fall in nor leave, Euler's steps left the circle at every step size, at exactly the rate its factor gives, while Runge-Kutta's stayed near it (the tables above give both). And the game behaved as that rotation stepped by Euler's rule: simultaneous steps moved away from the solution, taking turns stayed in a band of distances round it, and the look-ahead step came in, while descent on the loss, at the same eta, settled.",
    "The limits. Every field here is linear, with one rest point; a field that is not linear can have several, and closed loops that paths are drawn to, which no trace and determinant decide. The game is bilinear (f is x times y, a straight line in each player's number when the other's is held), the textbook case: on it the look-ahead step is proved to converge (Gidel and others, 2019) and taking turns to stay bounded without converging (Bailey, Gidel and Piliouras, 2019); a real GAN has players with many numbers, curved objectives and noisy slopes, and the repairs carry over only in part (the look-ahead step needs two looks at the slopes per step, the price paid here for coming in). And Runge-Kutta's advantage at the same work is measured on one smooth field; on a field that changes abruptly a smaller Euler step can be the cheaper choice.",
];

pub const EXTENSION: [&str; 3] = [
    "What this establishes: in the plane and for these fields, whether a field comes from a loss shows in two measurements (the curl, the sum round a loop), and it decides whether plain steps can circle; how a field is stepped is a second choice, with its own error. What remains open here: fields that are not linear (several rest points, closed loops that attract), fields learned from data, and fields on a curved space.",
    "Part 2 of this page, still to come, learns a small field that carries one set of points to another, in flat space and held to a sphere, and asks what a flow cannot do: a flow that can be run backward cannot tear one piece into two.",
    "Optimization explorer, the page before, followed gradient fields under different rulers: its paths were flows of the bowl's gradient field under a [metric|metric]. A ruler P turns the step -g into -P g, which in general is not itself a gradient field; but the loss still falls along it (its rate of change is -g . P g, below zero wherever g is not zero, for any ruler that gives every nonzero step a positive length, which is what a ruler is), so those paths could not circle either. The repairs on the game are two of several studied for training GANs; averaging the iterates of taking turns is another (Gidel and others, 2019), not measured here.",
];

pub const SUMMARY: [&str; 3] = [
    "The question was what decides where a state goes, and whether it must head downhill. The field decides, and it need not be downhill on anything: two of the five fields are minus the gradient of a loss, with no curl and a sum round a loop of zero, and three turn, with a sum equal to their curl times the area. Whether paths fall into the rest point or leave it was read off two numbers of the field's matrix, its trace and determinant, before looking at the paths, and the paths agreed.",
    "Following a field is a choice of its own: on the rotation, whose exact paths are circles, Euler's step spirals out at every step size by a factor known in advance, and a fourth-order Runge-Kutta step stays near the circle, nearer than Euler's even when both look at the field the same number of times.",
    "As machine learning meets it: gradient descent on a loss settles; two players each descending their own slope of f = x y follow a rotation, and stepping both at once, Euler's rule on it, carries them away from the solution. Taking turns keeps them circling in a band of distances, neither in nor out; the look-ahead step brings them in, at twice the looks per step.",
];

